Tenmaku Sway

Ingredients: NEW JACK SWING / SWINGBEAT + J-POP + 432Hz RELAXING MEDITATIVE STYLES + Synth  
Creative Catalyst: It was first performed at a wedding between two rival circus dynasties to stop a blood feud.

Tenmaku Sway sounds like the street-level swagger of 1991 Harlem swingbeat dropped into zero gravity and wrapped in acoustic velvet. The aggressive, metallic drum quantization of New Jack Swing is pulled back to a languid mid-tempo crawl, tuned strictly to the somatic frequency of 432 Hz. Beneath weightless Japanese pop chord changes—rich with major-seventh suspensions and shimmering pentatonic flourishes—lie plush FM synthesizers, dampened brass stabs, and singing bowls that ring out with the physical tension of acrobatic rigging. High-register J-pop vocal runs, typically hyperactive, are transformed into elongated, breathy sighs timed to the rhythmic arc of a pendulum.

